Falsifies `generational_table::verification::a_removed_name_never_resolves_again`.

The defect: the generation bump is deleted from `remove`. Taking the slot is what frees the
entry, so the bump reads as bookkeeping for a counter nobody consults, and every lookup of a
removed name still fails correctly until the slot is reused.

Expected to fail: the harness's `assert_ne!(new_name, name)`, where the reused slot mints the
name the dead entry had. That is a stale `ThreadId` resolving to a later occupant, which is the
whole reason this table is generational rather than an array of `Option`.

diff --git a/src/lib.rs b/src/lib.rs
--- a/src/lib.rs
+++ b/src/lib.rs
@@ -248,7 +248,6 @@
     pub fn remove(&mut self, name: EntryName<T>) -> Option<T> {
         let slot = self.slot_of(name)?;
         let value = self.slots[slot].take()?;
-        self.generations[slot] = self.generations[slot].saturating_add(1);
         self.live -= 1;
         // Removing anything below the top leaves a hole, which costs a walk nothing to skip.
         // Removing the top one means the bound can come down, past however many holes are under it.
